  "design_review": {
    "candidate": "FirstError one-time publication",
    "source_revision": "ccd0d3b00a3350eb4c769e9331f97d6233f25e0e",
    "scope": "Only the planner error latch changes. C68 observer, caches, output ordering, terrain, feature execution and worker budget stay the same.",
    "prior_behavior": "A Mutex<Option<String>> is locked by every check and failure publisher. Successful replay takes that mutex even while the value remains None. The first insertion wins; error checks clone the retained String.",
    "candidate_behavior": "A private FirstError owns OnceLock<String>. fail uses set, retaining the first successful publication and completing before return. check gets and clones the published value without acquiring a mutex on the normal successful path.",
    "concurrency_review": [
      "Publication races retain one of the original concurrent failures, as did mutex insertion races; caller-start order was not a promised ordering.",
      "A check overlapping an unfinished publication can return success, linearizing before publication. This cannot erase an error or overwrite the retained first value.",
      "Each View error calls fail synchronously before returning its fallback. The subsequent same-thread replay check must observe publication.",
      "try_for_each joins started evaluations before the final Planner::check. The final check therefore observes every completed failure publication even if another worker checked while publication was in progress.",
      "No lazy initializer closure or failing computation is stored in OnceLock: only an already-created String moves into the cell. No new reset/retry semantics or owner cycles.",
      "String cloning and original message propagation are unchanged after publication. Retirement remains inside the same planner destruction path."
    ],
    "new_tests": [
      "Sixteen racing publishers all observe the same retained original failure; later failure cannot replace it and a new job starts empty.",
      "Publication is observed by another thread while the publishing worker is deliberately still live, before the scoped join."
    ],
    "evidence": "Existing C53 stack samples contain actual Planner::check pthread mutex wait paths on both standard seeds (42/73 leaf observations); production latch unchanged through C68. This is evidence of possible contention, not time attribution. C68 explicitly leaves this latch outside its lookup buffer.",
    "validation_pending": [
